  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-subgaussian-variable-and-vector": [
      "## Definition\n\nUse the real random variable convention of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]] and the expectation convention of [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. A real random variable $X$ is **centered** when it is integrable and $\\mathbb E X=0$. A vector random element $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_d)$ in $\\mathbb R^d$, for an integer $d\\ge1$, is centered when each coordinate is integrable and $\\mathbb E Z_j=0$. For $u,z\\in\\mathbb R^d$, write $\\langle u,z\\rangle=\\sum_{j=1}^d u_jz_j$ and $\\|u\\|_2=(\\sum_{j=1}^d u_j^2)^{1/2}$, using the Euclidean convention in [[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]].\n\nFor $\\sigma^2\\ge0$, a centered real random variable $X$ is **sub-Gaussian with proxy $\\sigma^2$** if\n$$\\mathbb E e^{\\lambda X}\\le e^{\\lambda^2\\sigma^2/2}\\qquad\\text{for every }\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nA centered vector random element $Z\\in\\mathbb R^d$ is **sub-Gaussian with proxy $\\sigma^2$** if\n$$\\mathbb E e^{\\lambda\\langle u,Z\\rangle}\\le e^{\\sigma^2\\lambda^2\\|u\\|_2^2/2}\\qquad\\text{for every }u\\in\\mathbb R^d\\text{ and }\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R.$$\n\nFor $\\nu^2\\ge0$ and $b>0$, a centered real random variable $X$ is **sub-exponential with parameters $(\\nu^2,b)$** if\n$$\\mathbb E e^{\\lambda X}\\le e^{\\lambda^2\\nu^2/2}\\qquad\\text{whenever }|\\lambda|\\le1/b.$$\n\nThe parameters $\\sigma^2$ and $\\nu^2$ are moment-generating-function upper-bound proxies; they are not asserted to equal the variance. The vector condition controls every one-dimensional projection and does not assert independence of coordinates."
    ],
    "thm-hoeffding-independent-bounded-sums": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $n\\ge1$ and let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be independent integrable real random variables on one probability space. Suppose finite real numbers $a_i\\le b_i$ satisfy $a_i\\le X_i\\le b_i$ almost surely for every $i$. Put $V=\\sum_{i=1}^n(b_i-a_i)^2$ and $S=\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\mathbb E X_i)$. For every $t>0$, if $V>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P(|S|\\ge t)\\le 2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{2t^2}{V}\\right),$$\nand if $V=0$ then $\\mathbb P(|S|\\ge t)=0$. The two one-sided tails are each bounded by $\\exp(-2t^2/V)$ when $V>0$."
    ],
    "thm-bernstein-independent-sums": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $n\\ge1$ and let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be independent centered square-integrable real random variables. Suppose $b\\ge0$ is finite and $|X_i|\\le b$ almost surely for each $i$. Put $v=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\operatorname{Var}(X_i)$. For $t>0$, if $v>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P\\left(\\left|\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\\right|\\ge t\\right)\\le 2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{t^2}{2(v+bt/3)}\\right),$$\nand if $v=0$ then this probability is $0$."
    ],
    "lem-epsilon-net-of-unit-sphere-has-cardinality-bound": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d\\ge1$ be an integer and $0<\\epsilon<1$. There is a finite set $\\mathcal N\\subseteq S^{d-1}$ such that every $u\\in S^{d-1}$ has some $v\\in\\mathcal N$ with $\\|u-v\\|_2\\le\\epsilon$, and\n$$|\\mathcal N|\\le(1+2/\\epsilon)^d.$$\nHere $S^{d-1}=\\{u\\in\\mathbb R^d:\\|u\\|_2=1\\}$ by [[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]. Such a set is called an $\\epsilon$-net of the sphere."
    ],
    "lem-doob-martingale-differences-inherit-coordinate-oscillations": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $n\\ge1$, let $Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n$ be independent random elements with laws $\\mu_i$ on measurable spaces $(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$, and let $\\mu=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^n\\mu_i$. Let $f:\\prod_{i=1}^nS_i\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable with $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$. Suppose finite deterministic $c_i\\ge0$ satisfy\n$$|f(x_1,\\ldots,x_{i-1},x_i,x_{i+1},\\ldots,x_n)-f(x_1,\\ldots,x_{i-1},x_i',x_{i+1},\\ldots,x_n)|\\le c_i$$\nfor every $i$, every fixed choice of the other coordinates, and every $x_i,x_i'\\in S_i$. For each $i$, define $M_i$ by integrating $f$ over the unrevealed product coordinates, using the measurable finite versions given by Fubini; set $M_0=\\int f\\,d\\mu$ and $M_n=f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)$. Put $\\mathcal F_i=\\sigma(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_i)$ and $D_i=M_i-M_{i-1}$. Then $M_i$ is integrable and $\\mathcal F_i$-measurable, and for every $A\\in\\mathcal F_{i-1}$,\n$$\\int_A D_i\\,d\\mathbb P=0.$$\nMoreover, there are finite $\\mathcal F_{i-1}$-measurable endpoints $A_i,B_i$ such that $A_i\\le0\\le B_i$, $A_i\\le D_i\\le B_i$ almost surely, and $B_i-A_i\\le c_i$ almost surely. No choice axiom is used in this product-integral construction."
    ],
    "thm-subexponential-independent-sums": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be independent centered real random variables, with $X_i$ sub-exponential with parameters $(\\nu_i^2,b_i)$ as defined in [[def-subgaussian-variable-and-vector]], where $n\\ge1$ and $b_i>0$. Put $v=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\nu_i^2$, $b=\\max_{1\\le i\\le n}b_i$, and $S=\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i$. For $t>0$, if $v>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P(|S|\\ge t)\\le2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac12\\min\\!\\left\\{\\frac{t^2}{v},\\frac{t}{b}\\right\\}\\right),$$\nand if $v=0$ then $\\mathbb P(|S|\\ge t)=0$."
    ],
    "thm-finite-subgaussian-maximum-and-epsilon-net": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $Y_1,\\ldots,Y_m$ be centered sub-Gaussian real random variables with a common proxy $\\sigma^2\\ge0$, where $m\\ge1$. No independence is assumed. For $t>0$, if $\\sigma^2>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P\\left(\\max_{1\\le j\\le m}|Y_j|\\ge t\\right)\\le2m\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{t^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right),$$\nand if $\\sigma^2=0$ the probability is $0$.\n\nLet $Z$ be a centered sub-Gaussian vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with proxy $\\sigma^2$, where $d\\ge1$. For $0<\\epsilon<1$, let $\\mathcal N$ be the sphere net supplied by [[lem-epsilon-net-of-unit-sphere-has-cardinality-bound]]. Then for $t>0$, if $\\sigma^2>0$,\n$$\\mathbb P(\\|Z\\|_2\\ge t)\\le2(1+2/\\epsilon)^d\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(1-\\epsilon)^2t^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right),$$\nand if $\\sigma^2=0$ this probability is $0$. The deterministic reduction is $\\|z\\|_2\\le(1-\\epsilon)^{-1}\\max_{u\\in\\mathcal N}|\\langle u,z\\rangle|$ for every $z\\in\\mathbb R^d$."
    ],
    "thm-mcdiarmid-bounded-differences": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be independent random elements, meaning measurable maps ([[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]), with laws $\\mu_i$ given by pushforward ([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]). Put $\\mu=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^n\\mu_i$ on the finite product sigma-algebra ([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]). Let $f:\\prod_{i=1}^nS_i\\to\\mathbb R$ be product-measurable with $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$. Suppose finite deterministic constants $c_i\\ge0$ satisfy\n$$|f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i,\\ldots,z_n)-f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i',\\ldots,z_n)|\\le c_i$$\nfor every fixed setting of the other coordinates and every $z_i,z_i'$. The independent product-law theorem makes $f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)$ integrable with expectation $\\int f\\,d\\mu$. Put $V=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i^2$. For $t>0$, if $V>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P\\bigl(f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)-\\mathbb E f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)\\ge t\\bigr)\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{2t^2}{V}\\right),$$\nand if $V=0$ this probability is $0$. The same bound holds for the lower tail, so the two-sided probability is at most $2e^{-2t^2/V}$ when $V>0$."
    ],
